<HelpLayout title="Get the most out of Folio AI" active="get-the-most" description="Go beyond the basics — use Instructions, document uploads, web search, and model selection to get sharper, more on-brand results from Folio AI.">
  <p>
    Once you're comfortable generating and editing decks, a handful of features help Folio produce
    sharper, more relevant, and more on-brand results. This page walks through the four that make the
    biggest difference: <strong>Instructions</strong>, <strong>document uploads</strong>,
    <strong>web search</strong>, and <strong>model selection</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2>Instructions</h2>
  <p>
    Instructions let you give Folio standing guidance that applies to <em>every</em> deck you work
    on — so you don't have to repeat yourself in each prompt. Think of them as a system prompt for
    your account: rules about tone, formatting, terminology, and brand that Folio follows
    automatically.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Set your tone and style</strong> — for example, "always write in a concise, executive voice" or "avoid jargon."</li>
    <li><strong>Lock in brand and formatting rules</strong> — preferred fonts, color usage, how to title slides, or how to format numbers and dates.</li>
    <li><strong>Add domain context</strong> — describe your company, product, or audience once, and Folio keeps it in mind everywhere.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Open <strong>Instructions</strong> from the Folio side panel, write your guidance in plain
    language, and save. From then on it's applied to every generation and edit until you change it.
  </p>
  <HelpMedia src="/help/get-the-most-1.png" caption="The Instructions panel where you add standing guidance applied to every deck." />

  <h2>Upload documents</h2>
  <p>
    Give Folio the source material it needs by uploading your own documents. Folio reads them and
    uses their content as context for the slides it generates or edits — so your decks are grounded
    in your real data rather than generic filler.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>From your local machine</strong> — drag in reports, spreadsheets, PDFs, images, or an existing deck straight from your computer.</li>
    <li><strong>From a secured SharePoint</strong> — connect to your organization's SharePoint and pull in documents directly, without downloading them first.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Folio uses uploaded files only as context for your prompt. Upload a research report and ask Folio
    to turn it into a summary deck, or drop in a data file and have Folio build the supporting charts.
  </p>
  <HelpMedia src="/help/get-the-most-2.png" caption="Uploading documents from a local machine or a connected SharePoint." />

  <h2>Web search</h2>
  <p>
    When your prompt needs information Folio doesn't already have, enable <strong>web search</strong>
    and the agent will fetch additional, up-to-date information from the web to inform its work.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pull in current facts and figures</strong> — recent market data, statistics, or news that isn't in your uploaded files.</li>
    <li><strong>Research a topic on the fly</strong> — ask Folio to build a deck about a subject and let it gather the background it needs.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Toggle web search on for prompts that benefit from external context. For sensitive or fully
    self-contained work, leave it off so Folio relies only on your deck and uploaded documents.
  </p>
  <HelpMedia src="/help/get-the-most-3.png" caption="Enabling web search so the agent can fetch information from the web." />

  <h2>Model selection</h2>
  <p>
    Folio lets you choose the model behind the agent so you can balance depth against speed for the
    task at hand.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>More reasoning power</strong> — pick the stronger model for complex decks, nuanced restructuring, or work that needs careful, multi-step thinking.</li>
    <li><strong>Faster and leaner</strong> — pick the lighter model for quick edits, simple generations, and iterating rapidly when speed matters more than depth.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Switch models from the side panel whenever you like — there's no wrong choice, just the right
    trade-off for what you're doing right now.
  </p>
  <HelpMedia src="/help/get-the-most-4.png" caption="Choosing between a higher-reasoning model and a faster, leaner one." />

